A1. A coin-collector at Peter's expense. ~ PAUL
A5. Romulus and Remus sang their Mammy Song to this. ~ WOLF
A9. Galloping gas-bag. ~ BLIMP
A14. Farmer's unit of measurement to compute his landed wealth. (Ed. Note: What wealth?) ~ ACRE
A15. This pitcher used to go over nicely in the spring. ~ EWER
A16. This enables an artist to keep up his painting. ~ EASEL
A17. A line of ties. ~ RR
A18. To do this, the left is right. ~ DEAL
A20. A dark subject common to Scheherazade and the Caliph. ~ ARAB
A22. Tra's singing partner. ~ LA
A23. This point is generally brought up by a kicker. ~ TOE
A25. Verbal crutch used by limping orators. ~ ER
A26. A gnasty little gnipper. ~ GNAT
A27. What Jill did before she tumbled. ~ RAN
A28. A Norwegian ghost-writer. ~ IBSEN
A30. What all Holy Rollers make up together. ~ SECT
A31. Posted. ~ SENT
A32. It's hard to keep on this street. ~ EASY
A33. The last letters of a beheaded German poet. ~ EINE
A34. Hams are always seen on display here—waiting for eggs. ~ STAGE
A35. A channel swept by white-caps (abbr.). ~ ST
A36. Renown. ~ ECLAT
A38. A holdup on the bridge. ~ GIRDER
A39. Sunday jazz. ~ PSALM
A40. Nuts put squirrels in these just to get even. ~ CAGES
A41. China lilies with halitosis. ~ ONIONS
A43. Which reminds us, the Chinese and Japs aren't the only ones who've been fighting over this. ~ CHINA
A44. Horace Greeley'd be surprised to see the modern young man going this way. ~ UP
A46. Roebuck's mate. ~ SEARS
A47. In Russia it's a five-year one. We're for Stalin that long ourselves. ~ PLAN
A48. When she holds this card, suggest a goulash. ~ MENU
A50. You'll find a branch of the fork at this point. ~ TINE
A51. A strong follower of Gandhi. ~ GOAT
A52. A slicker for a waffle. ~ SYRUP
A53. Principal quality of the knight with a thousand I's. ~ EGO
A54. To the wonder of everyone, Willie's skin did this after his Thanksgiving dinner. ~ HELD
A55. Casual utterance of a Greek cow. ~ MU
A56. Cleopatra had a bite of this before she went out. ~ ASP
A57. A state of Concord (init.). ~ NH
A58. This is twice the size of the French Quarter. ~ DEMI
A59. The town that would rather burn than listen to Nero's music. ~ ROMA
A61. Unappreciated Icemen (abbr.). ~ UI
A62. You can't do this without raising a complaint. ~ DEMUR
A64. Try this cutoff: use the usual clip. ~ SNIP
A66. A bonny place. ~ BRAE
A68. To eat out. ~ ERODE
A69. Hardy perennial of the human breast. ~ HOPE
A70. These fish are always thinking up some new twist just to be different. ~ EELS

D1. Picnic, swimming, drop-in or political—they're all wet. ~ PARTIES
D2. A stage imitator of the ordinary pedestrian. ~ ACROBAT
D3. City on the Euphrates. Now in its 2000th year of depression. ~ UR
D4. What the heroine did to save her ace. ~ LED
D5. The beach flapper will soon do this with a smile. ~ WEAR
D6. Pussy-cat's first mate. ~ OWL
D7. An article of Fifi D'Orsay's. ~ LE
D8. Where Fifi picked it up. ~ FRANCE
D9. The cop's round. ~ BEAT
D10. Collegiate still (abbr.). ~ LAB
D11. This is so nearly his, we'll let him have it. ~ IS
D12. It takes a good mixer to make this. ~ MELANGE
D13. A persistent grower in the south. ~ PLANTER
D19. Poet's way of breaking even. ~ EEN
D21. This puts a bite in cheese. ~ RAT
D24. In a word, this is a typical feminine conclusion. ~ ESS
D26. Not quite a gentleman. ~ GENT
D27. What an editor who possesses enthusiasm in any volume does. ~ READS
D29. A sty. ~ EYESORE
D30. The country that produced that fast pair of twins. ~ SIAM
D31. This describes a flowing line. ~ STREAMY
D33. Wings. ~ ELLS
D34. The board of public highways. ~ SIGN
D37. Peaches are put in these, taken for a ride, and land on the shelf. ~ CANS
D38. What most people do after eating. ~ GAIN
D39. When he floated off on the deep river she accompanied him on this. ~ PIANO
D40. There's some talk about this. ~ CHAT
D41. Popular display place of the Belgian beach pajama (Belgian spelling). ~ OSTENDE
D42. A horse caller. ~ NEIGHER
D43. Covered by an investment. ~ CLAD
D44. A movie without the go-on-home kiss would be this. ~ UNUSUAL
D45. Whelp! Whelp! (plur.). ~ PUPPIES
D47. Any shine can do this. ~ POLISH
D49. It seems an age. ~ ERA
D51. This sets her off, him back, and the jeweler up. ~ GEM
D52. Bizarre result of a gathering of our national figures. ~ SUM
D54. Where you find the gang when it starts hailing. ~ HERE
D55. Pine. ~ MOPE
D58. This fell down but didn't go boom. ~ DUD
D59. The unluckiest girl got caught in this kind of tide with her new bathing suit. ~ RIP
D60. A high-powered Lincoln. ~ ABE
D63. Doubtful middle-western state (abbr.). ~ MO
D65. Sound made by a yes-man in reverse. ~ NO
D67. What a Scotchman adds to a treat when his turn comes. ~ RE


